      "name": null,
      "problem": "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(\\pi ,0)?$",
      "worked_solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(\\pi ,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2}y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2xy)\\text{ }i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\ -∇f(\\pi ,0) & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot 0)\\text{ }i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{\\pi }^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $i-{\\pi }^{2}\\,j.$",
