      "dom_id": "question-130277",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2(xy+1)^{3/2}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,1)?$",
      "worked_solution": "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(xy+1)^{3/2})\\,j \\\\  & =3y\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,i+3x\\sqrt{xy+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =3(1)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,i+3(3)\\sqrt{(3)(1)+1}\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+18\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $6\\,i+18\\,j.$",
